<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songhai Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Songhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Songhai Empire was a West African state centred on Gao on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, which expanded in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a>. It controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and the trans-Saharan trade and became one of the largest states in African history. It was defeated by a Moroccan invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> in 1591.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songhai-empire.jpg" alt="Songhai Empire" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Songhay_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Between the 9th and 3rd centuries BCE several groups formed the Songhai identity around Kukiya: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorko">Sorko</a>, who fished and carried goods by boat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, the Gao, who hunted river animals, and the Do, who farmed the fertile land. Before the 10th century horse-riding Songhai speakers subjugated them, and by the 10th century the Songhai chiefs had established Gao as a small kingdom controlling the trade routes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> conquered Gao around 1300 and held it until about the 1430s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited the town in 1353. As Mali weakened, the Songhai reasserted control and formalised their independence, probably under Sunni Muhammad Dao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sonni Ali took power in 1464, honouring the traditional religion his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dendi">Dendi</a> mother had taught him, which earned him the reputation of a tyrant among Islamic scholars. He annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in 1468 and starved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> into surrender after a seven-year siege, and he repelled attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> and conquered the Dogon. Under him the Songhai surpassed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in area, wealth and power. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-baru">Sonni Baru</a> was overthrown by one of his father's generals, Muhammad Ture, known as Askia the Great.</p><p>Askia organised the conquered territory, kept a full-time corps of warriors, built mosques and schools, and made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He centralised the administration, built canals, and introduced a system of weights and measures. In 1528 his children revolted against him, and after 1531 the empire declined through plots and civil wars. In 1591 Sultan Ahmad I al-Mansur of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadi dynasty</a> sent an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a>, with thousands of arquebusiers. The Songhai were routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> despite their numbers, and Judar sacked Gao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and Djenne. The Saadi dynasty soon let the region splinter, and Songhai nobles moved south to found smaller kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The empire's wealth rested on overland trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and river trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, above all gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> exchanged for salt, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a> and slaves sent north for horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and cloth. Under Sonni Ali dikes improved irrigation. Society was ordered by clans that decided occupations, with noblemen at the top, then freemen and traders, and prisoners of war and enslaved people at the bottom, working mainly in agriculture. Askia divided the territory into military zones under governors, and criminal justice followed Islamic principles, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> in towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and Djenne. Timbuktu was the educational capital, and a revival of Islamic scholarship took place at its university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Songhai Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, the southern branch under Abu Bakr ibn Umar helped spread Maliki Islam through the Sahel, contributing to the Islamisation of the western Sudan and shaping the religious character of the later Mali and <b>Songhai</b> empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Kano paid tribute to the Kanem-Bornu Empire from about the mid-fifteenth century, and <b>Songhai</b> forces under Askia Muhammad probably campaigned in Hausaland around 1513, although the extent of Songhai control is debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their trade linked Dagbon with the Fezzan, Egypt and the Bight of Benin, and the kingdom exchanged salt and kola nuts with the Mossi kingdoms, the Mali and <b>Songhai</b> empires and the Hausa Bakwai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population included Mandinka, Soninke, Fulani, <b>Songhai</b> and Tuareg communities, which encouraged cultural exchange and bound the outlying regions into one imperial economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the conquest of <b>Songhai</b> in 1591, gold from the Niger valley arrived in Marrakesh, earning Ahmad al-Mansur the name al-Dhahabi, the Golden, though the flow fell short of his hopes within a few years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…lived in a town of their own beside the capital of Ghana, and the rulers of Mali and <b>Songhai</b> accepted the faith; along the coast of East Africa it became the religion of the Swahili towns; in the Indian Ocean it spread to the ports of India and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
